ref='An uncertainty interval needs a target, sampling model and assumptions. Main exercise: independent normal measurements and known positive population sigma. With mean 10 units, sigma 2 units and n=16, sqrt(n)=4, SE=0.5, margin=1.96×0.5=0.98, and interval [9.02, 10.98]. Rounded critical value 1.96 gives approximately 95% theoretical repeated-sampling coverage. This interval concerns the population mean; it is not a range for 95% of individual values or a posterior probability attached to the fixed mean after observation. Unknown sigma requires a justified alternative method, and biased or dependent sampling is not repaired by arithmetic. Separate picture supplies B−A estimate −0.8 seconds and appropriate-method 95% bounds [−1.4, −0.2], entirely negative. Changed supplied bounds [−1.4, 0.3] include both signs and zero, not proof of equality. Raw data and fitting details are absent: no SE, symmetric reconstruction or refitting claimed. Neither example establishes every-request speed, causality or practical value. Code validates finite nondegenerate arithmetic, not real sampling assumptions.'
